\section{Threshold Regime Analysis}\label{sec:theory_scaling}

\subsection{Entity Construction as MBR Decoding}\label{sec:mbr_theory}

Entity-level construction selects $\hat{y}_{\textup{MBR}} = \{e \in E_\cup : \hat{p}(e) > \beta/(\beta{+}1)\}$ under balanced set-overlap utility; $\beta{=}1$ recovers majority vote (Proposition~\ref{prop:construction_mbr}; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}; Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_proof}).
Signal-derived weights $w_i$ yield quality-weighted MBR: $\hat{p}_w(e) = \sum_{i} w_i \, \mathbf{1}[e {\in} y_i] / \sum_{i} w_i$; weighting improves when $w_i$ correlates with instance quality but degrades otherwise (LP on SciERC: $\rho{\approx}0.2$; \S\ref{sec:prescriptive}; Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_connection}), consistent with utility degradation on structured outputs \cite{eikema2025structured}.
We select $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ at the elbow of the compression curve, where LP-best selection advantage transitions from $+1.5$\,pp (compressed) to $+4.6$\,pp (non-compressed; Appendix~\ref{app:fewnerd_epsilon}).

\subsection{Scaling and Inverse Scaling}\label{sec:scaling}

Oracle F1 follows $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$ ($R^2 > 0.999$; Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_scaling}): Few-NERD ($c{\approx}0.61$) scales comparably to reasoning tasks \cite{brown2024monkeys}, SciERC converges more slowly ($c{\approx}0.35$), yet confidence-based aggregation recovers ${<}1$\,pp, a persistent gap.
The gap persists across scales and families: scaling from 8B to 14B FT increases oracle headroom ($+1.2$\,pp) with minimal greedy gain ($+0.9$\,pp), and GPT-5.5 (200 Few-NERD instances, zero-shot) exhibits the same pattern: 50\% degeneracy, SJ $\rho{=}0.346$, MV $+1.4$\,pp yet 6.8\,pp oracle headroom unrealized.\footnote{Of the 10.1\% of GPT-5.5 samples producing empty entity sets, 88\% correspond to gold-empty instances; true parse failures affect only 1.2\% of samples (19/1{,}600) and shift all reported metrics by ${<}0.5$\,pp. See Appendix~\ref{app:frontier_sc}.}
Degeneracy is model-family-dependent rather than scale-dependent (Qwen2.5: $31.7$--$46.2$\% vs.\ Qwen3: $13.0$\% vs.\ GPT-5.5: $50$\% on Few-NERD; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig_oracle_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Oracle $F_1$ as a function of sample count $N$, fitted with $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$. All fits $R^2 > 0.999$. Horizontal dashed lines: greedy ($T{=}0$) F1. The ceiling grows monotonically with $N$, but the correlation-selection gap (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}) limits how much is actually recoverable.}
\label{fig:oracle_scaling}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Inverse scaling under adaptive thresholds.}
Increasing $N$ \textit{decreases} construction F1 in 40 of 58 transitions ($p{<}0.05$; Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).
Methods using $\theta{=}2/N$ are most affected ($-3.09$\,pp at $N{=}8$, $-6.90$\,pp at $N{=}16$ vs.\ greedy on Few-NERD); majority vote ($\theta{=}\tfrac{1}{2}$) reduces the delta to $-0.87$/$-0.60$\,pp, reflecting severity reduction, not immunity (Table~\ref{tab:theta_sweep}).
Inverse scaling concentrates in non-degenerate instances ($11.1$\% vs.\ $1.3$\% for degenerate; OR${=}0.109$; Appendix~\ref{app:degen_inverse}).
Few-shot demonstrations improve QE (CoNLL SJ $\rho$ $.30{\to}.44$) but not construction (all n.s.; Appendices~\ref{app:fs_error},~\ref{app:zs_vs_fs}).

\subsection{Degeneracy Decomposition}\label{sec:theory}

\noindent\textbf{Degeneracy Decomposition.}\label{thm:degeneracy_bound}
For any aggregation method $A$: $\mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1(A)] \leq (1 - D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}}$,
where $D$ is the degeneracy rate and $G_{\textup{nd}}$ the oracle gap on non-degenerate instances.
For signal-based methods with alignment $\alpha \in [0,1]$, this tightens to $(1{-}D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}} \cdot \alpha$ (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:bound_proof}).

\noindent The bound is an intentionally loose \textit{necessary condition test}: when $D{\to}1$ or $\alpha{\to}0$, no aggregation method can improve over greedy, regardless of implementation.
It identifies \textit{where} aggregation fails, screening for favorable conditions, rather than quantitatively predicting gains.
Across 16 configurations, all gains respect the bound; the low recovery ratio (3--12\%; Figure~\ref{fig:bound}) reflects its role as a screening test, not a tight estimate.
$\alpha$, not $D$, is the binding constraint ($\alpha{=}0.000$--$0.121$; $8$--$33{\times}$ tighter), and this conclusion is robust to signal combination (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}), verifier-based construction (\S\ref{sec:verifier}), and $\theta_{\text{crit}} \in [0.60, 0.80]$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_bound}
\caption{SC gain vs.\ degeneracy across 16 configurations (SciERC-specific $\alpha$). Red: signal-adjusted bound ($\alpha{=}0.068$); vertical dashed: $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$; horizontal dotted: $\varepsilon{=}0.3$\,pp. All positive gains fall within the bound (right panel: ratio to weak bound, all ${<}0.13$).}
\label{fig:bound}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Bound looseness.}
Two sources of looseness dominate: (1)~entity independence assumption (split-half $r{=}0.395$--$0.578$; Table~\ref{tab:fk_independence}); (2)~$\alpha$ estimated conservatively as instance minimum. These explain the 3--12\% recovery ratio, but the conclusion that $\alpha{\to}0$ is the binding constraint remains robust across all tested configurations.

MV improves F1 only if marginal-entity precision exceeds $F_1^{\textup{gr}}/2$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:entity_selection_bound}; Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_crit}).
Table~\ref{tab:alpha_crit} validates: at $N{=}8$, no dataset meets this condition ($\Delta$ from $-2.74$ to ${\approx}0$\,pp); CoNLL ($\beta_{\textup{crit,eff}}{=}1.148 {>} 1$) makes MV gain structurally impossible.

Oracle F1 follows $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$ ($R^2 > 0.999$; Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_scaling}): Few-NERD ($c{\approx}0.61$) scales comparably to reasoning tasks \cite{brown2024monkeys}, SciERC converges more slowly ($c{\approx}0.35$), yet confidence-based aggregation recovers ${<}1$\,pp, a persistent gap.
The gap persists across scales and families: scaling from 8B to 14B FT increases oracle headroom ($+1.2$\,pp) with minimal greedy gain ($+0.9$\,pp), and GPT-5.5 (200 Few-NERD instances, zero-shot) exhibits the same pattern: 50\% degeneracy, SJ $\rho{=}0.346$, MV $+1.4$\,pp yet 6.8\,pp oracle headroom unrealized.\footnote{Of the 10.1\% of GPT-5.5 samples producing empty entity sets, 88\% correspond to gold-empty instances; true parse failures affect only 1.2\% of samples (19/1{,}600) and shift all reported metrics by ${<}0.5$\,pp. See Appendix~\ref{app:frontier_sc}.}
Degeneracy is model-family-dependent rather than scale-dependent (Qwen2.5: $31.7$--$46.2$\% vs.\ Qwen3: $13.0$\% vs.\ GPT-5.5: $50$\% on Few-NERD; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_selection_gap}
\caption{The correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$). Left: moderate QE correlation (Spearman $\rho$ up to 0.38). Right: negligible selection gains (best-of-$N$ F1 at or below greedy). Dashed line indicates greedy baseline.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Quality estimation $\neq$ output selection.}
We attribute this gap (Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}) to a fundamental distinction: instance-level confidence signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} among $N$ candidates is best for a given input.
When per-sample variance is low relative to instance-level difficulty---as in NER, where the model often produces $N$ similar extractions---even perfect quality estimation cannot discriminate among near-identical candidates.

\paragraph{Implications for test-time scaling.}
This correlation-selection gap has direct consequences for test-time scaling in IE \cite{brown2024monkeys, snell2024scaling}.
Scaling inference compute through repeated sampling assumes a selector can identify the best sample.
Our results show that for structured prediction, existing confidence signals serve as quality \textit{estimators}---useful for flagging unreliable predictions---but not as effective \textit{selectors}.
Closing this gap likely requires signals that capture per-sample rather than per-instance variation, such as trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers} or pairwise comparison models.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Task Complexity and Signal Robustness}
\label{sec:complexity_robustness}

\paragraph{Task difficulty as a boundary condition.}
Signal effectiveness varies systematically with task difficulty.
On CoNLL (greedy micro F1${>}$0.91), EM leads all signals ($\rho{=}0.451$); on SciERC NER (micro F1$\,{\approx}\,$0.66), SJ overtakes EM ($\rho{=}0.357$ vs.\ 0.257); on SciERC RE, where 44\% of instances score zero F1, all signals converge to $\rho{\approx}0.25$.
The NER--RE difference is statistically confirmed: Fisher's $z$-test comparing SJ $\rho$ between NER ($\rho{=}0.357$) and RE ($\rho{=}0.211$) yields $z{=}2.31$, $p{=}0.021$, confirming statistically significant task-dependent degradation.

\paragraph{Per-relation diagnostic.}
RE signal degradation is relation-specific.
\textsc{Feature-Of} has near-zero correlation ($\rho{=}0.004$), while \textsc{Hyponym-Of} achieves $\rho{=}0.646$.
The primary bottleneck is entity span errors, which account for 55.5\% of RE false negatives---a prerequisite failure that consistency signals cannot detect, as they measure agreement among equally incorrect extractions.

\paragraph{Capability stratification.}
SJ effectiveness scales with structural complexity: on SciERC instances with 6+ entities, $\rho{=}0.547$, compared to $\rho{=}0.415$ for 1--2 entities.
Similarly, RE instances with ${\geq}3$ relation triples ($n{=}81$) yield substantially stronger correlations (Voting $\rho{=}0.630$, SJ $\rho{=}0.502$), demonstrating that within the model's capability range, increased output complexity amplifies structural signal advantage.
The SJ advantage over logprob remains stable across sentence-length quartiles ($\Delta\rho{\approx}0.08$--$0.20$), and three independent complexity proxies (entity count, sentence length, relation density) show weak mutual correlation ($|r|{<}0.15$), indicating a genuinely multi-dimensional benefit rather than a single confound.

\paragraph{Method boundary: WNUT-17.}
WNUT-17 establishes where the approach breaks down.
SJ achieves $\rho{=}0.062$ ($p{=}0.106$, not statistically significant), with 54\% of instances at zero F1.
Log-probability shows a strong \textit{negative} correlation ($\rho{=}{-}0.411$): the model is confidently wrong on novel entities, a calibration failure that no post-hoc consistency signal can remedy.
This is a boundary of the method, not a limitation of the datasets.

